<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Latvia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Latvia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Latvia, officially the Republic of Latvia, is a country in the Baltic region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, one of the three Baltic states, with its capital at Riga. After centuries of Teutonic, Swedish, Polish-Lithuanian and Russian rule, the independent Republic of Latvia was established on 18 November 1918, breaking away from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>. A coup in 1934 set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlis-ulmanis">Kārlis Ulmanis</a>. At the outset of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> it was forcibly incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, then occupied by Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1941 and by the Soviets again in 1944, forming the Latvian SSR for 45 years. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singing-revolution">Singing Revolution</a> began in 1987, and independence was restored on 21 August 1991.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Interwar defence depended on a small conscript army and a defensive alliance with <b>Latvia</b> from 1923, but no ally could protect Estonia against the great powers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Republic of Estonia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic of <b>Latvia</b> was born in the turmoil at the end of the First World War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…a Soviet republic, challenged the Soviet Union directly and encouraged independence movements in the other republics; the peaceful resistance of January 1991 and the Baltic Way of 23 August 1989, a human chain of some two million people across Lithuania, <b>Latvia</b> and Estonia, became symbols of non-violent struggle.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 1920s it signed treaties with Estonia and <b>Latvia</b> and had its own diplomats until 1923.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ukrainian-ssr">Ukrainian SSR · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In some post-Soviet states, laws banning communist symbols make a public performance of the Soviet anthem illegal: in Ukraine since 2015 it can carry up to five years in prison, and <b>Latvia</b> and Lithuania adopted similar laws.</blockquote><a href="/songs/state-anthem-of-the-soviet-union">State Anthem of the Soviet Union</a></li><li><blockquote>Some two million people joined hands across Estonia, <b>Latvia</b> and Lithuania on the 50th anniversary of the Nazi-Soviet Pact.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-23">23 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
